<https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#TSD_Art5> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Trade secret protection does not apply where disclosure is required for the exercise of the right to information. Directly engaged when a data subject invokes GDPR Art.15(1)(h) or AI Act Art.86 for an explanation of an automated medical decision — controller cannot use TSD Art.2 to block all causal explanation (C-203/22 §§60-70)."@en .
<https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#AIAct> <https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#coversArticle> <https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#AIAct_Art10> .
<https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#GDPR> <https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#coversArticle> <https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#GDPR_Art9> .
<https://onto.example.org/medical-ai-legal#Tension_EPO_GDPR_Disclosure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\r\n        EPC Art.83 requires public disclosure of training data structure to maintain patent validity.\r\n        GDPR Art.15(1)(h) + C-203/22 require actionable explanations to data subjects.\r\n        The EPO disclosure may inadvertently establish a baseline that strengthens GDPR access rights,\r\n        eroding TSD Art.2 trade secret shields that might otherwise limit GDPR Art.15 responses.\r\n        TSD Art.9 judicial confidentiality measures do not apply to EPO prosecution, leaving no\r\n        mechanism to contain the disclosure baseline once it enters the public patent record.\r\n    "@en .
